1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,420
The seven-member crew of America's 113th Space Shuttle mission

2
00:00:06,421 --> 00:00:10,820
exit the crew quarters on the third floor of the Operations and Checkout Building

3
00:00:10,821 --> 00:00:12,880
at Kennedy Space Center in Florida.

4
00:00:15,380 --> 00:00:18,320
They take their traditional walk to the transporter van

5
00:00:18,321 --> 00:00:20,360
that will carry them to the launch pad.

6
00:00:21,460 --> 00:00:25,040
We have booster ignition and liftoff of Space Shuttle Columbia

7
00:00:25,041 --> 00:00:29,200
with a multitude of national and international space research experiments.

8
00:00:30,860 --> 00:00:35,440
They lift off aboard America's first space shuttle, the 22-year-old Columbia.

9
00:00:36,880 --> 00:00:41,160
The astronauts spend the next 16 days in orbit conducting dozens of experiments

10
00:00:41,161 --> 00:00:43,140
dedicated to improving life on Earth.

11
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:49,840
Finally, each one makes their preparations to reenter the Earth's atmosphere and return home.

12
00:00:50,920 --> 00:00:53,840
This is amazing. It's really getting really bright out there.

13
00:00:54,660 --> 00:00:55,360
Yep.

14
00:00:55,700 --> 00:00:57,780
Yeah, you definitely don't want to be outside now.

15
00:00:57,781 --> 00:00:59,980
Looks like a black race.

16
00:01:01,620 --> 00:01:05,380
Only minutes from landing, with friends and families waiting to greet them,

17
00:01:06,140 --> 00:01:07,320
disaster strikes.

18
00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:10,240
My fellow Americans,

19
00:01:10,800 --> 00:01:13,340
this day has brought terrible news

20
00:01:13,341 --> 00:01:16,280
and great sadness to our country.

21
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:19,160
At 9 o'clock this morning,

22
00:01:20,240 --> 00:01:21,480
Mission Control in Houston

23
00:01:21,481 --> 00:01:24,860
lost contact with our space shuttle Columbia.

24
00:01:24,861 --> 00:01:31,320
A short time later, debris was seen falling from the skies above Texas.

25
00:01:33,040 --> 00:01:34,100
The Columbia's lost.

26
00:01:38,140 --> 00:01:44,640
Having flown in the Columbia, the most difficult emotional part of that for me

27
00:01:44,641 --> 00:01:48,340
was looking at my family, my wife and my children,

28
00:01:48,600 --> 00:01:52,060
and their emotional response to that loss.

29
00:01:52,061 --> 00:01:58,760
and then transferring that feeling to how terrible it must be for the families

30
00:01:58,761 --> 00:02:00,320
of the people that were on board the shuttle.

31
00:02:01,060 --> 00:02:04,240
You have a spectacular team that's still in what they can do,

32
00:02:05,040 --> 00:02:07,880
but they're trying to manage a vehicle which cannot be made safe.

33
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:12,100
It's a fantastically dangerous and vulnerable and critical machine.

34
00:02:13,100 --> 00:02:17,380
With the loss of Columbia, the world is reminded that a successful mission

35
00:02:17,381 --> 00:02:21,400
depends on the performance of millions of parts, systems, and people.

36
00:02:22,060 --> 00:02:28,960
With two exceptions, all of America's shuttle missions have met those unwavering conditions,

37
00:02:29,640 --> 00:02:31,960
and Columbia blazed the trail.

38
00:02:33,720 --> 00:02:39,980
Soon after NASA was formed in 1958, it began testing precursors to today's space shuttle.

39
00:02:41,880 --> 00:02:46,580
What has motivated people who are interested in spaceflight from the very beginning

40
00:02:46,581 --> 00:02:49,760
is the ability to take off like an airplane,

41
00:02:50,460 --> 00:02:53,560
fly into hypersonic and orbital velocities,

42
00:02:53,920 --> 00:02:57,420
go up into orbit, fly in orbit, and come back and land like an airplane.

43
00:02:57,520 --> 00:02:59,340
That's what everybody wants, and to do that repeatedly,

44
00:02:59,540 --> 00:03:01,360
just like we do in airliners today.

45
00:03:02,680 --> 00:03:06,020
But by 1961, with the space race accelerating,

46
00:03:06,580 --> 00:03:09,080
President John Kennedy had a more immediate goal.

47
00:03:09,081 --> 00:03:13,280
Of landing a man on the moon and returning him safely to the Earth.

48
00:03:13,281 --> 00:03:20,140
beating the Russians to the moon meant using more readily available, single-use only rockets and spacecraft.

49
00:03:22,480 --> 00:03:28,460
But seven years later, NASA's budgets were being cut, due in part to the Vietnam War.

50
00:03:29,980 --> 00:03:34,280
Its future would depend on finding a more economical means of exploring space.

51
00:03:34,281 --> 00:03:43,400
So by the summer of 1969, as Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin became the first human beings to explore the moon,

52
00:03:44,100 --> 00:03:48,060
plans for a reusable space vehicle had been revived.

53
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:57,960
The concept that NASA really championed was essentially two fully reusable vehicles.

54
00:03:58,960 --> 00:04:05,000
As this early NASA animation illustrates, the idea was to mate a pair of winged manned spacecraft.

55
00:04:06,380 --> 00:04:11,300
The larger, more powerful vehicle would boost the other to near-orbital velocities,

56
00:04:11,800 --> 00:04:16,420
then separate and return to a conventional runway landing back on Earth.

57
00:04:17,560 --> 00:04:21,200
The smaller vehicle would then fire its own engines to reach orbit.

58
00:04:22,380 --> 00:04:24,620
The problem with that, it was exceptionally expensive.

59
00:04:24,621 --> 00:04:28,020
And the budget constraints said that's not going to really be possible

60
00:04:28,021 --> 00:04:32,160
because you would have to develop two full reusable spacecraft.

61
00:04:33,240 --> 00:04:38,060
But as plans continued to develop, America was growing more entrenched in Vietnam

62
00:04:38,061 --> 00:04:41,300
and struggling with racial and economic tensions.

63
00:04:42,460 --> 00:04:47,880
Many voters and politicians alike believed the space program should be abandoned altogether

64
00:04:47,881 --> 00:04:50,620
in favor of solving problems at home.

65
00:04:50,621 --> 00:04:56,920
Some of President Richard Nixon's closest advisors, however, convinced him the shuttle must be built.

66
00:04:58,420 --> 00:05:03,880
John Ehrlichman talked about how he had a conversation with Nixon one day in which he says,

67
00:05:03,940 --> 00:05:08,180
you know, with this reusable vehicle that can go up into orbit and then return,

68
00:05:09,120 --> 00:05:15,160
we can go up and capture Soviet satellites and bring them back.

69
00:05:16,040 --> 00:05:19,660
And Nixon thought that was very cool and wanted to do that.

70
00:05:19,661 --> 00:05:23,800
Now, the shuttle's never been used for that purpose, but that was a selling point.

71
00:05:24,820 --> 00:05:27,420
Nixon would hear one other compelling argument as well.

72
00:05:28,420 --> 00:05:35,540
Casper Weinberger, who was at that point in time the second person at the Office of Management and Budget in the White House,

73
00:05:35,541 --> 00:05:47,180
made the case in the summer of 1971 that we really needed to approve this new vehicle that NASA was championing, this space shuttle,

74
00:05:47,181 --> 00:05:53,680
because we needed to show that our best days are not behind us,

75
00:05:53,800 --> 00:05:57,140
that we could do something that is for the benefit of all humanity.

76
00:05:58,080 --> 00:06:02,700
And he sent a memo into the president in which he made this case,

77
00:06:03,200 --> 00:06:05,940
and Nixon wrote across the top of it,

78
00:06:06,040 --> 00:06:07,120
I agree with Cap.

79
00:06:07,500 --> 00:06:09,700
It's time to do something about this.

80
00:06:10,180 --> 00:06:12,700
On April 21, 1972,

81
00:06:13,620 --> 00:06:19,320
Apollo 16 commander John Young was exploring the moon on NASA's next-to-last lunar landing

82
00:06:19,321 --> 00:06:22,880
when mission control transmitted big news from Earth.

83
00:06:24,440 --> 00:06:31,000
The outcast space budget gets to 277 to 60, which includes a vote for the shuttle.

84
00:06:31,240 --> 00:06:32,660
I don't want to do that.

85
00:06:32,780 --> 00:06:36,020
I don't think he needs that shuttle. My bad. You'll see.

86
00:06:37,800 --> 00:06:40,520
Congress had just approved funding for the space shuttle.

87
00:06:40,521 --> 00:06:45,080
Young was jubilant, though he couldn't possibly know that nine years later

88
00:06:45,081 --> 00:06:48,340
he'd serve as commander of the shuttle's maiden space voyage

89
00:06:48,341 --> 00:06:51,580
But before that day came

90
00:06:51,581 --> 00:06:54,720
the technological challenges of building the shuttle

91
00:06:54,721 --> 00:06:57,020
would nearly ground it forever

92
00:06:57,021 --> 00:07:01,720
If the shuttle's three main engines pumped water instead of fuel

93
00:07:01,721 --> 00:07:06,040
they would drain an average-sized swimming pool every 25 seconds

94
00:07:06,041 --> 00:07:11,040
Space Shuttle Columbia will return on Modern Marvels.

95
00:07:14,720 --> 00:07:17,500
When today's astronauts climb aboard the space shuttle,

96
00:07:18,140 --> 00:07:22,040
they enter a vehicle designed very much like the one Congress approved back in 1972.

97
00:07:24,260 --> 00:07:26,540
The crew lives and works in the orbiter.

98
00:07:27,500 --> 00:07:30,100
A combination rocket, spacecraft, and airplane,

99
00:07:30,600 --> 00:07:34,340
it is 122 feet long and has a 78-foot wingspan.

100
00:07:34,341 --> 00:07:45,421
The shuttle's three main engines, located at the rear of the orbiter, are fueled by a million and a half pounds of liquid hydrogen and liquid oxygen, stored in the

101
00:07:45,422 --> 00:07:47,800
154-foot-tall orange external tank.

102
00:07:49,300 --> 00:07:56,640
When the three main engines ignite, it sort of jolts you around a little bit, kind of like riding a 10-speed down a gravel road or something like that.

103
00:07:56,641 --> 00:08:04,560
A pair of 149-foot-long solid rocket boosters ignite, along with the main engines at liftoff.

104
00:08:05,840 --> 00:08:12,620
The two boosters produce 44 million horsepower, the equivalent of nearly 15,000 locomotives.

105
00:08:13,400 --> 00:08:17,100
When the solids ignite, it's kind of like riding a dirt bike down a railroad track.

106
00:08:17,160 --> 00:08:18,380
It gives you a pretty good shake.

107
00:08:19,780 --> 00:08:24,520
Just over two minutes after liftoff, with the shuttle flying at 3,200 miles per hour,

108
00:08:25,140 --> 00:08:27,960
the boosters burn out and are jettisoned from the vehicle.

109
00:08:29,460 --> 00:08:32,560
There's a small jolt as they are separated from the shuttle,

110
00:08:33,060 --> 00:08:34,920
and then they fall back in the ocean.

111
00:08:36,220 --> 00:08:40,860
The boosters are recovered, towed back to shore, and refurbished to fly again.

112
00:08:42,560 --> 00:08:46,760
52 miles above the Earth, the shuttle's main engine shut down

113
00:08:46,761 --> 00:08:49,080
as the external tank runs out of propellant.

114
00:08:49,900 --> 00:08:52,060
The tank then separates from the orbiter

115
00:08:52,061 --> 00:08:55,880
and breaks up as it falls back through the intense heat of the Earth's atmosphere.

116
00:08:56,920 --> 00:08:59,180
It is the only expendable part of the shuttle.

117
00:09:00,920 --> 00:09:05,920
The pilot then activates a set of smaller, liquid-fueled maneuvering engines to reach orbit.

118
00:09:07,320 --> 00:09:13,620
Eight minutes after liftoff, the orbiter is circling the Earth at 17,500 miles per hour.

119
00:09:16,220 --> 00:09:20,680
Returning home, it endures temperatures up to 2,900 degrees Fahrenheit

120
00:09:20,681 --> 00:09:22,760
as it re-enters the Earth's atmosphere.

121
00:09:24,860 --> 00:09:28,940
The orbiter must then glide, completely unpowered by engines,

122
00:09:29,380 --> 00:09:33,440
from halfway around the world to touch down on a specific runway.

123
00:09:35,080 --> 00:09:37,860
Such performance is still spectacular by today's standards.

124
00:09:38,620 --> 00:09:42,760
But the space shuttle was designed and built in the early 1970s,

125
00:09:43,160 --> 00:09:46,200
years before Americans enjoyed personal computers,

126
00:09:46,201 --> 00:09:50,180
digital display clocks, or even telephone answering machines.

127
00:09:50,680 --> 00:09:56,020
Building the shuttle was just as complicated as going to the moon in many respects.

128
00:09:56,400 --> 00:09:58,700
It required technologies that didn't exist.

129
00:09:59,240 --> 00:10:03,300
In some cases, NASA engineers didn't quite know how to get to those technologies.

130
00:10:03,420 --> 00:10:07,040
They could kind of roughly define what they needed, but they weren't sure how to get there.

131
00:10:08,800 --> 00:10:13,100
Developing those technologies would involve the contributions of over 50,000 Americans,

132
00:10:13,680 --> 00:10:17,460
working for hundreds of contractors in 47 different states.

133
00:10:17,461 --> 00:10:24,920
The solid rocket boosters were assembled, loaded with propellants, and tested by the

134
00:10:24,921 --> 00:10:26,840
Fiacol Company in Brigham City, Utah.

135
00:10:29,300 --> 00:10:34,880
They're made from weld-free, high-strength steel in a process called rolled ring forging.

136
00:10:36,280 --> 00:10:41,460
Each booster contains 11 segments that are joined together using high-strength steel pins.

137
00:10:41,461 --> 00:10:48,000
In New Orleans, Martin Marietta was at work on the huge external tank.

138
00:10:49,320 --> 00:10:54,080
Since the temperature of liquid hydrogen is 423 degrees below zero,

139
00:10:54,800 --> 00:10:57,360
the tank would be encased in spray-on insulation.

140
00:10:58,740 --> 00:11:03,840
This would prevent ice from accumulating on its surface in the humidity of its Florida launch site.

141
00:11:05,940 --> 00:11:09,680
The most complex job went to Rockwell International in Southern California.

142
00:11:09,681 --> 00:11:13,860
Rockwell would design and assemble the orbiter itself.

143
00:11:15,800 --> 00:11:19,000
Its Rocketdyne division was responsible for the shuttle's main engines.

144
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:24,900
In all, the shuttle would consist of 2.5 million parts,

145
00:11:25,540 --> 00:11:28,200
including more than 1,000 plumbing valves and connections,

146
00:11:28,960 --> 00:11:32,980
1,400 circuit breakers, and 230 miles of wire.

147
00:11:33,980 --> 00:11:37,680
I remember when I started on the program, I asked myself,

148
00:11:37,681 --> 00:11:40,500
is it possible to do this thing?

149
00:11:41,380 --> 00:11:42,620
Is it really possible?

150
00:11:42,740 --> 00:11:45,280
Because when you looked at all the things that have to happen

151
00:11:45,281 --> 00:11:47,200
and the time was allocated,

152
00:11:48,140 --> 00:11:51,200
emotionally you thought or intellectually you thought it's just not possible.

153
00:11:52,340 --> 00:11:55,680
But what we did is instead we just focused on the year at a time.

154
00:11:57,400 --> 00:12:00,380
On the morning of September 17, 1976,

155
00:12:00,960 --> 00:12:04,300
the Rockwell team treated the world to a glimpse of the future,

156
00:12:05,620 --> 00:12:06,960
a test model of the orbiter.

157
00:12:07,680 --> 00:12:11,780
one not intended for spaceflight, rolled out of their Palmdale, California plant.

158
00:12:12,900 --> 00:12:17,760
It was named Enterprise, after the starship in the television series Star Trek.

159
00:12:20,100 --> 00:12:26,900
In August of 1977, astronauts Fred Hayes and Gordon Fullerton would put Enterprise to the test.

160
00:12:28,380 --> 00:12:36,180
Their job was to release the 150,000-pound glider from the back of a specially equipped 747 jumbo jet,

161
00:12:36,181 --> 00:12:40,720
and with only one chance for success, attempt a landing.

162
00:12:42,040 --> 00:12:46,800
At 24,000 feet, Hayes fired the explosive bolts holding the aircraft together.

163
00:12:47,580 --> 00:12:52,060
And as expected, Enterprise lifted straight up as the jetliner veered away.

164
00:12:53,420 --> 00:12:58,960
Five minutes later, Hayes glided Enterprise toward the runway for what would be a perfect landing.

165
00:13:00,020 --> 00:13:04,000
But the test only proved the shuttle as a glider, not as a spacecraft.

166
00:13:04,960 --> 00:13:07,580
That test would be up to the shuttle Columbia,

167
00:13:08,180 --> 00:13:11,620
which was nearing completion and was plagued with two major problems.

168
00:13:13,440 --> 00:13:16,240
The first involved the shuttle's three main engines,

169
00:13:16,660 --> 00:13:18,660
the most complex engines ever developed.

170
00:13:20,520 --> 00:13:25,220
One of the difficult new requirements for this rocket engine

171
00:13:25,221 --> 00:13:26,900
is that it be reusable.

172
00:13:27,940 --> 00:13:31,580
In fact, it's to be reused for 55 flights.

173
00:13:31,581 --> 00:13:36,180
Previously, all rocket engines were expendable.

174
00:13:37,640 --> 00:13:42,220
In addition, the engines needed to be much lighter than prior rocket engines.

175
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:45,780
Yet for their weight, they had to be far more powerful,

176
00:13:46,600 --> 00:13:50,460
capable of generating 23 times as much power as Hoover Dam.

177
00:13:50,980 --> 00:13:55,920
Individual turbine blades were roughly the size of a postage stamp,

178
00:13:56,460 --> 00:13:59,360
and it developed 600 horsepower per blade.

179
00:13:59,361 --> 00:14:07,340
During testing, those turbine blades cracked and nozzles gave out under the extreme pressures and vibrations.

180
00:14:09,080 --> 00:14:15,720
It was later discovered that the grade of welding wire used on the engines was causing weaknesses in welded areas.

181
00:14:17,540 --> 00:14:23,520
By upgrading the wire and making other modifications, the engines began testing with consistent success.

182
00:14:23,521 --> 00:14:30,140
The second challenge that they had was a thermal protection system, or the TPS as it's called.

183
00:14:32,040 --> 00:14:36,060
The TPS protects the orbiter from burning up as it re-enters the atmosphere.

184
00:14:36,900 --> 00:14:38,520
It would consist of three materials.

185
00:14:39,840 --> 00:14:43,460
Reinforced carbon-carbon panels were installed on the nose and wing edges.

186
00:14:44,320 --> 00:14:46,560
The sections that encountered the highest temperatures.

187
00:14:48,160 --> 00:14:51,300
Most of the top side of the orbiter was considered least vulnerable

188
00:14:51,301 --> 00:14:53,540
and covered with a flame-resistant fabric.

189
00:14:55,140 --> 00:14:58,640
The greatest challenge was protecting the largest section of the Orbiter,

190
00:14:59,300 --> 00:15:02,500
especially the underside, which dissipates most of the heat.

191
00:15:05,120 --> 00:15:09,820
These areas would be protected by nearly 31,000 individually designed,

192
00:15:10,100 --> 00:15:12,540
lightweight, heat-resistant ceramic tiles.

193
00:15:14,620 --> 00:15:18,720
The problem was getting the tiles to bond to the Orbiter's aluminum skin.

194
00:15:18,721 --> 00:15:21,420
this tile is a ceramic

195
00:15:21,421 --> 00:15:24,260
and if you really look under a microscope

196
00:15:24,261 --> 00:15:27,360
it's probably 97% air

197
00:15:27,361 --> 00:15:28,800
there's nothing there

198
00:15:28,801 --> 00:15:32,700
it's sand in strands that have been

199
00:15:32,701 --> 00:15:35,480
heat treated and then a coating put on

200
00:15:35,481 --> 00:15:37,720
and when we put that tile

201
00:15:37,721 --> 00:15:39,080
attached to the vehicle

202
00:15:39,081 --> 00:15:42,000
it was breaking at a point

203
00:15:42,001 --> 00:15:44,120
where we didn't understand why it was breaking

204
00:15:44,121 --> 00:15:47,120
in March 1979

205
00:15:47,121 --> 00:15:50,420
a blue ribbon panel of experts from around the country

206
00:15:50,421 --> 00:15:53,340
began searching for a solution to the tile problem.

207
00:15:54,600 --> 00:15:57,860
At the same time, in order to maintain a processing schedule,

208
00:15:58,580 --> 00:16:01,200
NASA transported Columbia to the Kennedy Space Center

209
00:16:01,201 --> 00:16:03,220
with thousands of tiles still missing.

210
00:16:05,360 --> 00:16:08,800
Six months later, the experts determined that the solution

211
00:16:08,801 --> 00:16:11,440
was to add a special coating to the back of each tile,

212
00:16:12,120 --> 00:16:13,920
strengthening where it bonds to the orbiter.

213
00:16:13,921 --> 00:16:19,720
Workers at the Kennedy Space Center would spend until Christmas 1980

214
00:16:19,721 --> 00:16:26,480
Modifying, applying, and waterproofing the 30,759 tiles on the orbiter

215
00:16:26,481 --> 00:16:35,000
By the spring of 1981, Columbia, the most complex and expensive vehicle ever built

216
00:16:35,001 --> 00:16:36,720
Was ready to fly

217
00:16:36,721 --> 00:16:42,620
Columbia was named for a Boston sailing vessel

218
00:16:42,621 --> 00:16:44,020
captained by Robert Gray,

219
00:16:44,460 --> 00:16:48,260
who in 1790 completed the first U.S. circumnavigation of the globe.

220
00:16:49,180 --> 00:16:51,940
Space Shuttle Columbia will return on Modern Marvels.

221
00:16:55,640 --> 00:16:57,520
Prior to every space shuttle flight,

222
00:16:57,521 --> 00:17:00,920
a series of events take place at Florida's Kennedy Space Center

223
00:17:00,921 --> 00:17:04,180
that is nearly as spectacular as liftoff itself.

224
00:17:04,181 --> 00:17:10,920
The shuttle is rolled out of its processing hangar on a 96-wheel transporter

225
00:17:10,921 --> 00:17:15,240
and carried to the 525-foot-tall Vehicle Assembly Building,

226
00:17:15,800 --> 00:17:19,600
which was originally built to assemble Saturn V rockets during the Apollo era.

227
00:17:22,360 --> 00:17:27,500
The newly completed Columbia made this trip for the first time in November 1980.

228
00:17:28,240 --> 00:17:33,940
The first time Columbia rolled out of the processing facility after years of working there,

229
00:17:33,941 --> 00:17:38,060
and rolled over to the VAB, there were thousands of people out there

230
00:17:38,061 --> 00:17:42,000
just to watch that short trip between the processing facility and the VAB.

231
00:17:42,140 --> 00:17:45,740
All those technicians that glued tile on, all those engineers that wrote software,

232
00:17:45,900 --> 00:17:49,420
all those people that worked in logistics that procured parts,

233
00:17:50,060 --> 00:17:53,300
everybody felt that they were a member of the team.

234
00:17:55,080 --> 00:17:58,220
Waiting for every orbiter inside the vehicle assembly building

235
00:17:58,221 --> 00:18:01,260
are the solid rocket boosters and external tank,

236
00:18:01,261 --> 00:18:05,400
which had been raised to the vertical position and attached together.

237
00:18:07,100 --> 00:18:11,000
A pair of cranes lift the orbiter vertically toward the top of the building,

238
00:18:11,540 --> 00:18:12,980
high above the external tank.

239
00:18:14,560 --> 00:18:18,960
It's aligned with the tank and is gently lowered down and attached to it.

240
00:18:21,040 --> 00:18:26,820
Next, this slow-moving 6 million-pound vehicle called the Shuttle Crawler Transporter

241
00:18:26,821 --> 00:18:31,040
carries the spacecraft out of the building and toward the launch pad.

242
00:18:31,260 --> 00:18:37,100
Fully loaded, the crawler moves at one mile per hour.

243
00:18:38,600 --> 00:18:45,720
For its maiden voyage, mission STS-1, Columbia arrived at the pad on December 31, 1980,

244
00:18:46,460 --> 00:18:50,180
four months prior to launch, for a series of pre-launch tests.

245
00:18:50,840 --> 00:18:57,180
The spacecraft was nothing like we had ever checked out or launched before.

246
00:18:57,181 --> 00:19:05,760
Most of the checkout with the previous capsules or spacecraft were switches in the cockpit and manual activity.

247
00:19:06,720 --> 00:19:16,460
This brought in a computer complex on board and drove us to more computers in our ground checkout than we'd ever had before.

248
00:19:16,680 --> 00:19:20,600
So our engineers had become very proficient in computers.

249
00:19:20,601 --> 00:19:26,480
And for people that had grown up in the slide rule era, this was a challenge.

250
00:19:27,180 --> 00:19:34,240
The computers would play a key role in firing the shuttle's engines and igniting the solid rocket boosters.

251
00:19:35,160 --> 00:19:43,800
The Air Force was telling Congress that the shuttle will lose one out of 30 missions due to solid rocket boosters alone.

252
00:19:44,800 --> 00:19:51,820
They said, we're sorry to give you the news, but we are operating solid rocket boosters, and that's our record.

253
00:19:53,260 --> 00:19:56,280
So SDS-1 had a lot of unknowns.

254
00:19:56,980 --> 00:19:58,140
There was a lot of risk taken.

255
00:19:58,240 --> 00:20:02,880
The people who flew that really were quite courageous because there was no guarantee.

256
00:20:04,220 --> 00:20:07,600
Those people would include NASA's most experienced astronaut.

257
00:20:08,780 --> 00:20:13,220
John Young, a veteran of two Gemini missions as well as two Apollo missions,

258
00:20:13,740 --> 00:20:15,440
was selected to command Columbia.

259
00:20:17,060 --> 00:20:18,760
The pilot was Bob Crippen.

260
00:20:19,440 --> 00:20:24,920
Though he was an Apollo-era astronaut and had more than 4,000 hours of jet aircraft flying time,

261
00:20:24,921 --> 00:20:27,800
This would be his first space flight.

262
00:20:29,880 --> 00:20:36,860
On April 12, 1981, Young and Crippen posed for cameras during the astronauts' traditional pre-launch breakfast.

263
00:20:38,760 --> 00:20:46,260
In a few hours, they would be flying the first vehicle sent into space without the benefit of previously unmanned orbital testing.

264
00:20:48,360 --> 00:20:51,740
It was almost like Apollo again. We hadn't been in space.

265
00:20:52,320 --> 00:20:58,480
In 1981, when they flew Columbia, we had not sent humans into space since 1975, six-year absence.

266
00:20:58,800 --> 00:21:00,620
So everybody was excited about this.

267
00:21:00,920 --> 00:21:03,360
And then there were all these unknowns about this mission.

268
00:21:03,840 --> 00:21:05,320
Would the engine work properly?

269
00:21:05,640 --> 00:21:10,220
These shuttle tiles that were glued onto the underside of the orbiter, would that be successful?

270
00:21:10,940 --> 00:21:14,280
We've gone for main engine start. We have main engine start.

271
00:21:14,281 --> 00:21:25,080
...from America's first space shuttle, and the shuttle has cleared the tower.

272
00:21:27,780 --> 00:21:29,880
As Columbia made its ascent,

273
00:21:30,900 --> 00:21:33,660
Crippen and Young noticed debris flying past their window.

274
00:21:34,860 --> 00:21:39,640
It would later be determined that this was ice and insulation from the external tank,

275
00:21:40,360 --> 00:21:43,440
an occurrence that would become common on future flights.

276
00:21:44,280 --> 00:21:47,900
and prove fatal on Columbia's final flight.

277
00:21:48,920 --> 00:21:50,280
We see SIB set flight.

278
00:21:51,500 --> 00:21:52,280
Roger on a set.

279
00:21:55,480 --> 00:21:57,780
The rest of the ascent went flawlessly.

280
00:21:58,820 --> 00:22:04,560
Once in orbit, Young and Crippen's first test would be opening the 60-foot-long payload bay doors.

281
00:22:05,420 --> 00:22:07,660
But this procedure was more than a test.

282
00:22:08,160 --> 00:22:10,240
Okay, the fourth door's coming open now.

283
00:22:10,920 --> 00:22:11,620
Roger, copy.

284
00:22:11,621 --> 00:22:16,140
Inside the doors are radiators that help cool the shuttle

285
00:22:16,141 --> 00:22:20,820
So opening them as soon as possible would be a critical step in all shuttle missions

286
00:22:20,821 --> 00:22:23,740
The doors opened perfectly

287
00:22:23,741 --> 00:22:28,820
But when they did, Young and Crippen pointed cameras back toward the engine pods

288
00:22:28,821 --> 00:22:30,740
And saw a disturbing sight

289
00:22:30,741 --> 00:22:36,740
We do have a few tiles missing off of both of them

290
00:22:36,741 --> 00:22:42,380
off of the starboard pod, three tile and some smaller pieces,

291
00:22:42,900 --> 00:22:47,640
and off the port pod looks like, I see one full square,

292
00:22:47,860 --> 00:22:50,060
and it looks like a few little triangular shapes.

293
00:22:51,460 --> 00:22:56,280
NASA determined that since these were the less critical white tiles on the top side of the orbiter,

294
00:22:56,800 --> 00:22:58,980
as opposed to the black tiles on the underside,

295
00:22:59,760 --> 00:23:01,940
a safe reentry was still possible.

296
00:23:01,941 --> 00:23:07,260
assuming the tiles on the underside were not damaged as well.

297
00:23:08,680 --> 00:23:12,140
Nobody knew with certainty whether they were or not.

298
00:23:13,300 --> 00:23:17,680
Two days later, Columbia attempted to become the first winged spacecraft

299
00:23:17,681 --> 00:23:21,980
to re-enter the atmosphere and make a conventional runway landing.

300
00:23:22,860 --> 00:23:24,180
John, we're all riding with you.

301
00:23:26,500 --> 00:23:29,880
Back on Earth, a six-mile-long string of traffic

302
00:23:29,881 --> 00:23:34,280
was carrying half a million spectators to Edwards Air Force Base in California

303
00:23:34,281 --> 00:23:36,560
to witness the historic touchdown.

304
00:23:37,980 --> 00:23:40,500
While Columbia was enduring the searing heat of reentry,

305
00:23:41,160 --> 00:23:45,200
mission control would lose radio contact for approximately 20 minutes.

306
00:23:45,840 --> 00:23:48,300
And they lost it just as they were supposed to,

307
00:23:48,620 --> 00:23:52,100
and as you come out of that, they weren't sure that they would hear these guys again.

308
00:23:52,860 --> 00:23:58,580
There's concern that they knew some of those shuttle tiles had been popped off during the launch.

309
00:23:59,200 --> 00:24:00,280
Well, what would happen?

310
00:24:00,380 --> 00:24:02,920
Would there be a burn through of these individual places?

311
00:24:03,260 --> 00:24:04,820
Would the crew come back safely?

312
00:24:08,860 --> 00:24:10,440
What a way to come to California.

313
00:24:11,440 --> 00:24:13,100
They regain the telemetry.

314
00:24:13,320 --> 00:24:17,080
They hear a sonic boom as it comes below the speed of sound.

315
00:24:17,081 --> 00:24:22,400
They see it off in the distance, and Walter Cronkite literally breathes a sigh of relief,

316
00:24:22,740 --> 00:24:25,280
wipes his brow and says, they fade it.

317
00:24:28,580 --> 00:24:35,120
Soon after landing, John Young was supposed to remain on board for a brief medical exam.

318
00:24:37,120 --> 00:24:40,400
Instead, he came bounding out of the shuttle ahead of time.

319
00:24:41,380 --> 00:24:44,040
In a post-flight statement, he explained why.

320
00:24:44,280 --> 00:24:49,740
Everyone had said that they had shown, from an engineering and statistics standpoint,

321
00:24:50,260 --> 00:24:53,300
that it was impossible, impossible, mind you,

322
00:24:53,600 --> 00:24:57,340
that some of those critical tiles on the bottom of the vehicle would not fall off.

323
00:24:57,341 --> 00:24:59,140
But none of those tiles fell off.

324
00:24:59,420 --> 00:25:02,300
And it's all due to the individual effort of the American people.

325
00:25:05,000 --> 00:25:09,600
Over the next 15 months, Columbia would fly three more two-man missions

326
00:25:09,601 --> 00:25:12,760
to test the shuttle's various systems in different orbits.

327
00:25:15,400 --> 00:25:18,720
Its fifth flight, launched November 11, 1982,

328
00:25:19,560 --> 00:25:22,640
was considered the space shuttle's first fully operational mission

329
00:25:22,641 --> 00:25:27,060
and marked the first time a four-man crew ever flew into space.

330
00:25:27,340 --> 00:25:31,780
Columbia would soon be joined by three sister orbiters.

331
00:25:32,880 --> 00:25:36,020
Together, they would make space travel seem routine

332
00:25:36,021 --> 00:25:38,740
until disaster proved otherwise.

333
00:25:40,600 --> 00:25:43,260
Columbia's maiden voyage was made on the 20th anniversary

334
00:25:43,261 --> 00:25:47,680
of the first manned spaceflight by Soviet cosmonaut Yuri Gagarin.

335
00:25:48,720 --> 00:25:51,420
Space Shuttle Columbia will return on Modern Marvels.

336
00:25:51,421 --> 00:25:57,820
During the first 22 years of the space shuttle program,

337
00:25:58,400 --> 00:26:02,020
few astronauts spent more hours in orbit than Story Musgrave.

338
00:26:03,400 --> 00:26:06,200
His sixth and final mission was on board the Columbia.

339
00:26:07,080 --> 00:26:08,840
Launched in November of 1996,

340
00:26:09,660 --> 00:26:13,620
the mission distinguished him as the only person to ever fly on all five shuttles.

341
00:26:14,220 --> 00:26:18,380
And at age 61, he became the oldest person to ever fly in space

342
00:26:18,381 --> 00:26:21,060
until John Glenn flew on the shuttle Discovery.

343
00:26:21,420 --> 00:26:24,420
in 1998 at age 77.

344
00:26:25,620 --> 00:26:29,360
But to this astronaut, who joined NASA during the Apollo era

345
00:26:29,361 --> 00:26:33,320
and played key roles in developing several shuttle systems,

346
00:26:34,600 --> 00:26:38,740
flying the reusable spacecraft was anything but routine.

347
00:26:41,640 --> 00:26:46,780
The vehicle that we were getting into, it was simply frightening.

348
00:26:47,160 --> 00:26:51,240
I don't do this because it's dangerous, and I don't do it because it's risky.

349
00:26:51,420 --> 00:26:55,100
I do it because I'm a space person and I have no other way to get up there.

350
00:26:55,680 --> 00:26:59,120
I certainly would have rather gotten there by Mercury, Gemini, or Apollo.

351
00:27:00,460 --> 00:27:04,820
Vehicles that are ten times safer or a hundred times safer than the shuttle is.

352
00:27:06,120 --> 00:27:11,880
Yet throughout the early 1980s, the shuttle would continue with one successful mission after the next.

353
00:27:12,040 --> 00:27:18,880
Way out there on the end of the runway, the space shuttle Challenger, affixed atop a 747,

354
00:27:18,881 --> 00:27:25,180
is about to start on the first leg of a journey that would eventually put it into space in November.

355
00:27:25,400 --> 00:27:26,980
It's headed for Florida now.

356
00:27:28,480 --> 00:27:37,760
With the addition of three more orbiters, Challenger in 1983, Discovery in 1984, and Atlantis in 1985,

357
00:27:38,860 --> 00:27:42,100
NASA was pushing to launch shuttles as quickly as possible.

358
00:27:43,000 --> 00:27:46,000
Turn them around like airplanes initially was the thought.

359
00:27:46,001 --> 00:27:52,260
They found out very quickly that wasn't possible, but we could still fly maybe as many as 25 missions a year,

360
00:27:52,460 --> 00:27:53,700
you know, one every other week.

361
00:27:54,400 --> 00:27:59,040
And that was a very exciting prospect, and to get there you have to push very hard.

362
00:27:59,041 --> 00:28:04,920
And so all through 1985, the NASA administrator is pressuring the shuttle program to launch on schedule,

363
00:28:05,180 --> 00:28:09,560
and if you look at the number of launches that year, they're much greater than in any other year in the history of the program.

364
00:28:09,561 --> 00:28:13,640
1986 was expected to be equally as demanding,

365
00:28:14,080 --> 00:28:17,740
and Columbia was scheduled to fly the year's first mission in January.

366
00:28:19,300 --> 00:28:23,520
Despite pressure to launch, the mission set a record of seven postponements,

367
00:28:24,200 --> 00:28:28,840
four with a crew on board, which included U.S. Congressman Bill Nelson of Florida.

368
00:28:29,400 --> 00:28:37,880
Only after the fact did I find out some of the near misses that we had in those four scrubs.

369
00:28:37,881 --> 00:28:40,080
Everything is go. The orbit is on board computers.

370
00:28:40,240 --> 00:28:42,660
We have a cutoff.

371
00:28:43,900 --> 00:28:46,980
One scrub occurred just 31 seconds to launch

372
00:28:46,981 --> 00:28:49,380
when it was discovered that a fatigued technician

373
00:28:49,381 --> 00:28:53,240
had accidentally allowed 18,000 pounds of liquid oxygen

374
00:28:53,241 --> 00:28:55,620
to drain out of the external tank.

375
00:28:57,140 --> 00:28:59,660
Had we launched 31 seconds later,

376
00:28:59,840 --> 00:29:04,220
we would have not had enough fuel to get to orbital velocity,

377
00:29:04,221 --> 00:29:11,460
and therefore Hoot Gibson, our commander, would have had to put down a fully loaded spacecraft

378
00:29:11,461 --> 00:29:17,640
in an emergency landing on a 10,000-foot runway in Dakar, Senegal,

379
00:29:17,641 --> 00:29:22,400
where the end of the runway dropped straight off down a cliff into the Atlantic Ocean.

380
00:29:23,580 --> 00:29:28,840
Columbia finally lifted off on January 12, 1986 for a six-day mission.

381
00:29:28,841 --> 00:29:32,860
The space shuttle Challenger with teacher Krista McAuliffe

382
00:29:32,861 --> 00:29:36,500
was set to launch just four days after Columbia returned home.

383
00:29:36,840 --> 00:29:42,920
I don't think any teacher has ever been more ready to have two lessons in my life.

384
00:29:43,100 --> 00:29:47,320
I've been preparing these since September, and I just hope everybody tunes in.

385
00:29:47,500 --> 00:29:52,360
Krista McAuliffe was going to do this broadcast from space, this lesson from space.

386
00:29:52,440 --> 00:29:57,900
It was going to go literally around the world and be taught to students all over.

387
00:29:58,840 --> 00:30:03,940
Never before had two shuttle missions been scheduled to launch so close to each other.

388
00:30:04,780 --> 00:30:10,940
Weather and mechanical delays, however, pushed the Challenger launch back six days to January 28th.

389
00:30:12,320 --> 00:30:19,560
On that day, the temperature was a frigid 36 degrees, 15 degrees colder than any prior launch,

390
00:30:20,060 --> 00:30:22,640
and icicles could be seen forming on the launch pad.

391
00:30:22,641 --> 00:30:27,060
While the crew was having its pre-launch breakfast,

392
00:30:27,700 --> 00:30:30,040
engineers who had designed the solid rocket boosters

393
00:30:30,041 --> 00:30:34,300
were warning NASA that such temperatures could trigger a catastrophic failure

394
00:30:34,301 --> 00:30:36,120
in the booster's joint seals.

395
00:30:37,400 --> 00:30:41,240
The technical decision in those matters has got to be

396
00:30:41,241 --> 00:30:46,440
not just analytical data, but it has to be common sense data.

397
00:30:46,441 --> 00:30:49,680
Like foot and a half long icicles on the launch pad,

398
00:30:49,820 --> 00:30:52,040
you look at the icicles and you say no.

399
00:30:52,640 --> 00:30:54,900
What is this? What are we doing?

400
00:30:55,860 --> 00:30:56,840
And you don't go.

401
00:30:58,920 --> 00:31:00,560
But the shuttle did launch.

402
00:31:01,340 --> 00:31:04,060
And with school children the world over watching on television,

403
00:31:04,600 --> 00:31:07,870
the worst accident in the history of U.S.

404
00:31:07,871 --> 00:31:11,380
space exploration occurred 73 seconds later.

405
00:31:15,840 --> 00:31:20,100
The cause of the accident was blamed on a design flaw in the solid rocket boosters

406
00:31:20,101 --> 00:31:25,140
and on NASA management for putting money and expediency ahead of safety.

407
00:31:27,140 --> 00:31:31,120
The Challenger tragedy grounded the space shuttle for the next 32 months.

408
00:31:32,020 --> 00:31:37,280
Columbia and the two remaining orbiters were transported back to their California manufacturing facility

409
00:31:37,281 --> 00:31:40,620
for more than 200 safety improvements and modifications.

410
00:31:43,380 --> 00:31:46,420
There was a major redesign of the solid rocket boosters,

411
00:31:46,940 --> 00:31:49,260
and improvements were made to the shuttle's main engines.

412
00:31:50,100 --> 00:31:55,360
NASA also commissioned the building of the space shuttle Endeavour,

413
00:31:55,900 --> 00:31:58,340
which would make its maiden voyage in 1992.

414
00:32:00,260 --> 00:32:02,160
But perhaps more significantly,

415
00:32:02,920 --> 00:32:06,440
NASA's management structure, safety, and decision-making procedures

416
00:32:06,441 --> 00:32:08,520
were drastically overhauled.

417
00:32:09,580 --> 00:32:14,100
It put control of technical operations in the hands of the technicians again,

418
00:32:14,360 --> 00:32:18,040
and they would resist any interference by people that didn't know the technology.

419
00:32:18,620 --> 00:32:21,600
And so the launch control director was responsible for launches.

420
00:32:21,780 --> 00:32:24,840
Mission control was responsible for running missions, and that's the way it went.

421
00:32:25,100 --> 00:32:28,360
And I've been incredibly proud of that team, confident of that team.

422
00:32:29,540 --> 00:32:33,280
Throughout the 1990s, Columbia flew 16 missions,

423
00:32:33,960 --> 00:32:38,260
launching satellites, conducting hundreds of experiments to improve life on Earth,

424
00:32:38,820 --> 00:32:41,760
and helping us better understand the depth of our universe.

425
00:32:43,240 --> 00:32:47,700
On March 1, 2002, Columbia flew to the Hubble telescope,

426
00:32:48,040 --> 00:32:49,080
to pay a service call.

427
00:32:51,520 --> 00:32:54,460
Among other improvements, the crew replaced a camera

428
00:32:54,461 --> 00:32:56,500
with one that had twice the field of view

429
00:32:56,501 --> 00:32:58,420
and five times the resolution.

430
00:32:59,940 --> 00:33:01,280
Meanwhile, back on Earth,

431
00:33:01,660 --> 00:33:03,480
the crew for Columbia's next voyage

432
00:33:03,481 --> 00:33:06,960
was busy training for one of the largest science missions ever flown.

433
00:33:08,100 --> 00:33:10,440
It would prove to be Columbia's final journey.

434
00:33:11,860 --> 00:33:13,140
In June 1995,

435
00:33:13,740 --> 00:33:15,740
NASA delayed the launch of the shuttle Discovery

436
00:33:15,741 --> 00:33:21,100
after finding that woodpeckers had poked dozens of holes in the external fuel tank's foam insulation.

437
00:33:22,440 --> 00:33:25,140
Space Shuttle Columbia will return on Modern Marvels.

438
00:33:26,460 --> 00:33:29,200
The shuttle program opens space travel to the world.

439
00:33:30,340 --> 00:33:35,960
Columbia alone hosted astronauts from Germany, Canada, Italy, Ukraine, and Japan.

440
00:33:35,961 --> 00:33:48,680
In 1998, the first astronaut from Israel moved his family to Houston to begin training to fly on Columbia.

441
00:33:50,780 --> 00:33:58,220
Ilan Ramon, married with four children, was the son of a Holocaust survivor and a fighter pilot in the Israeli Air Force.

442
00:33:59,020 --> 00:34:01,620
His crewmates would become like a second family.

443
00:34:02,440 --> 00:34:03,360
They were.

444
00:34:04,280 --> 00:34:07,280
Commander Rick Husband, married with two young children.

445
00:34:08,080 --> 00:34:11,360
Columbia would be the second shuttle flight for this Air Force test pilot.

446
00:34:12,940 --> 00:34:16,240
Pilot Willie McCool, married with three children.

447
00:34:16,840 --> 00:34:19,880
He was a Navy fighter pilot who hadn't yet flown a shuttle mission.

448
00:34:22,060 --> 00:34:25,660
Payload Commander Michael Anderson, married with two children.

449
00:34:26,360 --> 00:34:29,000
This Air Force pilot had flown one prior shuttle mission.

450
00:34:29,001 --> 00:34:34,840
Mission Specialist Kalpan Achola, or K.C. to the rest of the crew

451
00:34:34,841 --> 00:34:39,920
Married with no children, this aeronautical engineer and pilot was born in India

452
00:34:39,921 --> 00:34:42,220
And flew one previous shuttle mission

453
00:34:42,221 --> 00:34:47,900
Mission Specialist David Brown, the only bachelor on the crew

454
00:34:47,901 --> 00:34:51,500
Was a physician, Navy pilot, and flight surgeon

455
00:34:51,501 --> 00:34:54,000
As well as a former circus performer

456
00:34:55,200 --> 00:34:57,540
Columbia would be his first shuttle flight

457
00:34:57,541 --> 00:35:03,440
Mission Specialist Laurel Clark, married with one child.

458
00:35:04,180 --> 00:35:07,960
She was also a pediatrician, a flight surgeon, and a Navy commander.

459
00:35:08,900 --> 00:35:10,900
This would be her first shuttle mission as well.

460
00:35:12,080 --> 00:35:15,960
Well, all the crew members were excited about being on the science mission,

461
00:35:16,200 --> 00:35:18,340
and that's true for any mission that flies in space.

462
00:35:18,440 --> 00:35:19,860
The crew's always excited to be there.

463
00:35:19,861 --> 00:35:25,460
But this one perhaps even more so, because it's one of the few that was purely dedicated to science.

464
00:35:26,460 --> 00:35:30,800
The crew's 16-day mission would require them to complete more than 80 experiments.

465
00:35:32,200 --> 00:35:36,900
They were designed to shed light on such areas as combating prostate and bone cancer,

466
00:35:38,020 --> 00:35:41,980
kidney stone formation, sleep patterns, and fire suppression.

467
00:35:44,560 --> 00:35:50,720
With a huge workload ahead of them, the excited crew lifted off at 10.39 a.m.

468
00:35:50,721 --> 00:35:52,320
on January 16, 2003.

469
00:35:52,321 --> 00:36:00,320
By initial appearances, the launch in ascent seemed normal, and Columbia reached its planned orbit.

470
00:36:02,180 --> 00:36:06,560
The following day, however, NASA engineers reviewed video of the launch.

471
00:36:07,460 --> 00:36:11,500
They discovered that 81 seconds after liftoff, at 60,000 feet,

472
00:36:12,380 --> 00:36:18,900
a chunk of foam from the external tank struck the protective carbon-carbon panel on the edge of the left wing.

473
00:36:18,901 --> 00:36:24,120
an area where temperatures reached nearly 3,000 degrees on reentry.

474
00:36:25,640 --> 00:36:29,820
Since Columbia's first flight, NASA was aware of foam striking the orbiter,

475
00:36:30,300 --> 00:36:32,820
but it had been dismissed as a serious safety threat.

476
00:36:34,200 --> 00:36:36,620
Yet this strike did concern some engineers.

477
00:36:37,920 --> 00:36:41,480
Though lightweight, the object was estimated to be three feet long

478
00:36:41,481 --> 00:36:45,120
and struck when the shuttle's speed was about 500 miles per hour.

479
00:36:46,560 --> 00:36:48,180
You know what a milk carton is?

480
00:36:48,181 --> 00:36:51,740
try something that weighs one and a half times a milk carton,

481
00:36:51,820 --> 00:36:53,680
it's in your face at 500 miles an hour.

482
00:36:53,900 --> 00:36:55,040
It's going to take your head off.

483
00:36:56,540 --> 00:36:58,820
While the crew was going about its work in orbit,

484
00:36:59,580 --> 00:37:01,440
NASA engineers had issued reports

485
00:37:01,441 --> 00:37:04,600
indicating the strike could have caused a breach on the edge of the wing,

486
00:37:05,120 --> 00:37:06,840
which would doom the shuttle on re-entry.

487
00:37:09,340 --> 00:37:12,400
However, these were considered worst-case scenario reports,

488
00:37:12,600 --> 00:37:16,360
and their managers concluded the strike was not serious enough to pose a danger.

489
00:37:18,180 --> 00:37:23,840
In footage recovered after Columbia's destruction, the crew appeared to relish their time in space.

490
00:37:24,660 --> 00:37:28,060
Good morning, Ken. Good morning to all.

491
00:37:28,061 --> 00:37:33,780
And a special good morning to my wife, Rona, the love of my life.

492
00:37:41,640 --> 00:37:42,340
Baseball.

493
00:37:42,520 --> 00:37:44,000
Hey, better, better, better, better, better, better.

494
00:37:44,440 --> 00:37:45,300
Zero G baseball.

495
00:37:48,180 --> 00:37:49,580
You're supposed to catch it, Elon.

496
00:37:53,580 --> 00:37:54,300
Bicycle kick.

497
00:37:57,480 --> 00:37:59,340
Wow, Willie, that was impressive.

498
00:38:00,180 --> 00:38:05,200
On the morning of February 1, 2003, the crew prepared to return home.

499
00:38:05,960 --> 00:38:09,320
There might be some plasma now.

500
00:38:10,440 --> 00:38:11,960
We see it out the front also.

501
00:38:12,200 --> 00:38:17,720
A little north of Hawaii, at an altitude of 75 miles,

502
00:38:18,180 --> 00:38:21,000
Columbia penetrated the outer fringes of the atmosphere.

503
00:38:22,000 --> 00:38:24,920
Soon after, an orange glow surrounded the orbiter

504
00:38:24,921 --> 00:38:28,880
as atmospheric friction began heating up the thermal protection system.

505
00:38:29,300 --> 00:38:32,160
This is amazing. It's really getting really bright out there.

506
00:38:33,000 --> 00:38:36,100
Yep. Yeah, you definitely don't want to be outside now.

507
00:38:36,840 --> 00:38:38,340
Looks like a blast for us.

508
00:38:39,720 --> 00:38:42,880
Twenty-three minutes prior to its scheduled landing in Florida,

509
00:38:43,560 --> 00:38:45,680
Columbia sailed just north of San Francisco

510
00:38:45,681 --> 00:38:49,720
when mission control monitors began showing the first hints of trouble.

511
00:38:51,340 --> 00:38:57,560
FYI, I've just lost four separate temperature transducers on the left side of the vehicle.

512
00:38:58,200 --> 00:39:01,520
There was a halt in the flow of temperature readings from the left wing.

513
00:39:03,140 --> 00:39:06,180
Since sensor glitches during reentry were not uncommon,

514
00:39:07,100 --> 00:39:10,100
Flight Director Leroy Cain was not yet overly concerned.

515
00:39:10,101 --> 00:39:17,900
over the next five minutes however more sensors went dead and the temperature in the left landing

516
00:39:17,901 --> 00:39:25,520
gears spiked 60 degrees the tires pressure on left outboard left inboard both tires

517
00:39:25,521 --> 00:39:30,860
and columbia houston we see your tire pressure messages and we did not

518
00:39:30,861 --> 00:39:32,420
Is it instrumentation, Max?

519
00:39:32,940 --> 00:39:33,640
I'll fight Max.

520
00:39:33,641 --> 00:39:34,420
Those are also off.

521
00:39:34,421 --> 00:39:35,121
Roger.

522
00:39:41,560 --> 00:39:44,240
Communication with the crew was suddenly lost.

523
00:39:44,900 --> 00:39:47,600
Columbia was 39 miles over north-central Texas,

524
00:39:48,140 --> 00:39:52,640
traveling 18 times the speed of sound, 16 minutes from its scheduled landing.

525
00:39:54,260 --> 00:39:57,000
Columbia, Houston, UHF comm check.

526
00:39:57,001 --> 00:40:02,700
While flight controllers tried to reestablish communication for several minutes,

527
00:40:03,800 --> 00:40:10,980
Texas and Louisiana residents were reporting mysterious loud noises and fireballs streaking through the sky.

528
00:40:12,160 --> 00:40:14,600
GC flight. GC flight.

529
00:40:15,500 --> 00:40:17,420
Fly GC. Lock the doors. Copy.

530
00:40:19,040 --> 00:40:24,360
At 9.29 a.m., flight director Kane declared a contingency,

531
00:40:25,240 --> 00:40:27,520
A NASA term meaning the shuttle had been lost.

532
00:40:28,340 --> 00:40:29,860
Mission control was locked down.

533
00:40:30,500 --> 00:40:32,620
Phone calls were not allowed in or out,

534
00:40:33,080 --> 00:40:36,780
as controllers began saving and downloading all of Columbia's flight data.

535
00:40:40,080 --> 00:40:43,220
Within minutes, a massive recovery effort was begun.

536
00:40:44,020 --> 00:40:49,020
It covered a 2,800-square-mile area from mid-Texas through Louisiana.

537
00:40:49,021 --> 00:41:00,918
Over the next several months, the Columbia Accident Investigation Board would conclude that superheated gases entered a breach in Columbia's left wing during re-entry

538
00:41:00,919 --> 00:41:04,000
and melted its internal aluminum structure.

539
00:41:05,340 --> 00:41:08,280
They blamed the breach on the foam strike during liftoff.

540
00:41:10,440 --> 00:41:17,520
The board conducted tests by shooting a piece of foam from a nitrogen-powered cannon at nearly 500 miles per hour.

541
00:41:19,020 --> 00:41:21,840
directly at a carbon-carbon panel.

542
00:41:23,240 --> 00:41:25,640
Even though the foam struck a glancing blow,

543
00:41:26,140 --> 00:41:28,400
it produced a hole 16 inches across,

544
00:41:28,800 --> 00:41:31,120
more than large enough to cause the breach.

545
00:41:34,100 --> 00:41:36,640
The board also made several recommendations.

546
00:41:37,680 --> 00:41:41,180
It called for modifications to the foam on the external tank

547
00:41:41,181 --> 00:41:42,660
to reduce debris strikes.

548
00:41:44,060 --> 00:41:46,660
It also recommended that astronauts be trained

549
00:41:46,661 --> 00:41:49,080
to repair the thermal protection system in orbit,

550
00:41:49,700 --> 00:41:54,320
and that better photographic coverage be provided between launch and ascent.

551
00:41:55,880 --> 00:41:59,800
The remaining space shuttles are expected to fly for at least another decade,

552
00:42:00,380 --> 00:42:03,720
as they continue to play a vital role for the International Space Station.

553
00:42:05,380 --> 00:42:08,140
While various replacements for the shuttle are under study,

554
00:42:08,860 --> 00:42:11,580
none are expected to come online in the near future.

555
00:42:11,581 --> 00:42:22,080
In the days following the disaster, a memorial service was held on the shuttle runway at Kennedy Space Center.

556
00:42:23,420 --> 00:42:27,760
Bob Crippen, Columbia's first pilot, talked about his old friend.

557
00:42:28,720 --> 00:42:36,420
I'm sure that Columbia, which had traveled millions of miles and made that fiery re-entry 27 times before,

558
00:42:36,421 --> 00:42:43,580
struggled mightily in those last moments to bring her crew home safely once again.

559
00:42:44,900 --> 00:42:46,520
She wasn't successful.

560
00:42:48,320 --> 00:42:54,060
Columbia and the other orbiters were all named after great explorer ships

561
00:42:54,061 --> 00:42:57,300
for that is their mission, to explore the unknown.

562
00:42:58,440 --> 00:43:04,260
Columbia was hardly a thing of beauty except those of us who loved and cared for her.

563
00:43:04,261 --> 00:43:06,660
Many said she was old and past her prime.

564
00:43:08,080 --> 00:43:12,440
Still, she had only lived barely a quarter of her design life.

565
00:43:13,440 --> 00:43:15,740
Columbia had a great many missions ahead of her.

566
00:43:17,060 --> 00:43:21,500
She, along with the crew, had her life snuffed out in her prime.

567
00:43:22,380 --> 00:43:25,960
There's heavy grief in our hearts, which will diminish with time,

568
00:43:26,680 --> 00:43:30,980
but it will never go away, and we will never forget.

569
00:43:34,260 --> 00:43:43,000
Hail Rick, Willie, Casey, Mike, Laurel, Dave, and Alain.

570
00:43:44,160 --> 00:43:45,000
Hail Columbia.

571
00:43:55,800 --> 00:43:57,480
Tonight on Modern Marvel's Wednesday.

572
00:43:57,960 --> 00:44:01,160
So, when you think of a warrior, does this kind of look come to mind?

573
00:44:01,400 --> 00:44:03,280
I mean, check out the protective headgear.

574
00:44:03,281 --> 00:44:05,760
And what about these super sharp woolly gauntlets?

575
00:44:06,000 --> 00:44:10,560
Seriously, though, this was standard army gear for some of the toughest fighters in World War II.

576
00:44:10,820 --> 00:44:14,960
The Winter Warriors on Modern Marvels, tonight at 8 on the History Channel.

